      <h2 id="stack">The stack</h2>
      <p>Every site I build runs on the same foundation. I choose tools that are fast, proven, and easy to maintain, with no server runtime to watch over. Versions and lockfiles are pinned in every repository, so <code>package.json</code> shows exactly what's running.</p>

      <h3>Core</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://www.11ty.dev" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Eleventy (11ty) v3 ESM</a></strong>: the static site generator. It turns Nunjucks templates, Markdown content, and JavaScript data files into flat HTML at build time. The config (<code>eleventy.config.js</code>) is ESM, with <code>"type": "module"</code> in <code>package.json</code>.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://mozilla.github.io/nunjucks/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Nunjucks</a></strong>: the templating language, with inheritance, partials, filters, and JavaScript data. I build layouts with <code>extends</code> and partials with <code>include</code>, so no template grows into a monolith.</li>
        <li><strong>Hand-written pages.</strong> Every page is custom-coded HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in its own template file, written for that business. There's no WordPress underneath, no page builder on top, and no component from a template marketplace.</li>
        <li><strong>Vanilla CSS</strong> with custom properties, <code>clamp()</code>, and <code>@container</code> queries where they help, and no framework. Organized partials are joined into one stylesheet at build time. Production never uses <code>@import</code>, because it causes request waterfalls.</li>
        <li><strong>Vanilla JavaScript</strong>, with no framework, no JS build step, and no hydration. It's progressive enhancement only: the site works fully without JavaScript, which makes a few interactions nicer.</li>
        <li><strong>Markdown</strong> for blog posts and long-form content, through 11ty's built-in markdown-it processor, with front matter for metadata. Content is plain text in <code>.md</code> files, not locked in a database.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Build-time plugins</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://www.11ty.dev/docs/plugins/image/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">@11ty/eleventy-img</a></strong>: makes AVIF, WebP, and JPEG versions with a width-based <code>srcset</code>, native lazy loading, and dimension hints that prevent layout shift (CLS).</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://www.11ty.dev/docs/plugins/rss/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">@11ty/eleventy-plugin-rss</a></strong>: builds the RSS and Atom feed for the blog.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sardine/eleventy-plugin-tinyhtml" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">@sardine/eleventy-plugin-tinyhtml</a></strong>: minifies HTML with careful whitespace handling.</li>
        <li><strong>eleventy-plugin-gen-favicons</strong>: builds the full favicon set (every device, every browser, and both dark and light variants) from one source file.</li>
        <li><strong>eleventy-auto-cache-buster</strong>: adds content-hashed query strings to CSS and JS references, so returning visitors get fresh files instead of stale ones.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom CSS-joining extension</strong>: registers <code>.css</code> as a template format in <code>eleventy.config.js</code>, reads the partials in a set order, and writes one minified stylesheet. The browser never sees an <code>@import</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom sitemap template</strong>: <code>sitemap.njk</code> loops through <code>collections.all</code>, leaves out assets and excluded pages, and writes a valid <code>sitemap.xml</code> with <code>priority</code> and <code>changefreq</code>. I don't use <code>@quasibit/eleventy-plugin-sitemap</code> or <code>@11ty/eleventy-plugin-sitemap</code>, because both have reliability problems with v3.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Outside services that run in the browser</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://pagefind.app" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Pagefind</a></strong>: static, client-side full-text search. It indexes at build time and serves the index as static JSON chunks, with no server. It runs in an <code>eleventy.after</code> hook, so the Cloudflare Pages build preset works without changes.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://web3forms.com" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Web3Forms</a></strong>: a form backend built for static sites. Submissions POST straight to its API, so I never run a server-side form handler. It uses a honeypot and rate limiting instead of a CAPTCHA.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://atomiks.github.io/tippyjs/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Tippy.js</a></strong> and <strong><a href="https://popper.js.org" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Popper</a></strong>: an accessible tooltip library for glossary terms. It loads site-wide and attaches itself to <code>[data-term]</code>, <code>[data-tooltip]</code>, and any link to the glossary, so a plain link gets a definition tooltip for free.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://leafletjs.com" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Leaflet</a></strong> and <strong><a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap</a></strong>: service-area maps on <code>/areas/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> pages and demo sites, with no Google Maps tracker, no API key, and no cookies. Tiles come straight from OSM. About 200 city coordinates in <code>_data/cityCoords.js</code> plot nearby communities, and names that repeat across states (Aurora, CO and IL; Smyrna, GA and TN) are matched by state.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://instant.page" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">instant.page</a></strong>: prefetches a link when the visitor hovers over it, so the next page opens almost at once. It's 1.5 KB, with no API.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Analytics</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://umami.is" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Umami</a></strong>: open-source, cookie-free analytics and the main source of traffic data. The dashboard shows top pages, referrers, devices, and events (such as taps on the phone number). It's included with every monthly plan: I host the dashboard and send each client a private live link on launch day.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a></strong>: server-side Real User Monitoring (RUM) for page views, referrers, devices, and real-world Core Web Vitals. It adds no client-side tracking and no cookies, it's GDPR-compliant by design, and it's also included with every monthly plan.</li>
        <li><strong>No advertising trackers by default.</strong> I don't install Google Analytics, the Facebook Pixel, the LinkedIn Insight Tag, the TikTok Pixel, or any other advertising tracker. They slow the site, they need a cookie banner, and most browsers block them anyway. I can add Google Analytics 4 if a client asks for it, though I don't recommend it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Hosting, DNS, and email</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://pages.cloudflare.com" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Pages</a></strong>: static hosting on a global Anycast CDN with more than 300 edge locations, automatic HTTPS through free managed SSL, HTTP/3 by default, and unlimited bandwidth on the Free tier. The build container runs <code>npx @11ty/eleventy</code> on every push to <code>main</code>. Uptime over the last 12 months: 99.99%.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare Workers</strong>, when needed: serverless edge functions for routing or small APIs. I use them sparingly, only when Web3Forms can't cover a need.</li>
        <li><strong>DNS</strong>: Cloudflare nameservers for clients I host fully. For clients who prefer to manage DNS at their current registrar, I send the exact records to add.</li>
        <li><strong>Your domain stays in your name.</strong> It stays at your registrar, registered to you and under your control, and I point its DNS at my hosting. I don't register domains for clients, so control of a domain never sits with me.</li>
        <li><strong>Email on your domain.</strong> I don't host email, but I set up the MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records for the provider you choose: Google Workspace ($7.20 per user a month), Microsoft 365 ($7.20 per user a month), or free forwarding to an inbox you already have, such as Gmail. This setup is part of launch, and I never charge to configure DNS.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Source control and deploys</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="https://github.com" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></strong>: every client site lives in a private repository, and the client can see the commit history on request. I sign commits with verified GPG keys.</li>
        <li><strong>CI/CD</strong>: the built-in Cloudflare Pages preset runs <code>npx @11ty/eleventy</code> on every push to <code>main</code>. Every other branch gets a preview deploy, and a rollback takes one click, so I don't keep a separate CI server.</li>
        <li><strong>Branch protection</strong>: <code>main</code> is protected, and merges must pass status checks (the build, plus Lighthouse CI on key templates).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="interactive-tooling">Other work on this site</h2>
      <p>This site is also my portfolio. Each piece below runs entirely in the browser, with no backend, so all of it works on a static host.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The eight <a href="/vs-wix/" class="inline-link">comparison pages</a></strong>: side-by-side looks at Wix, Squarespace, GoDaddy, Webflow, WordPress, AI builders, traditional agencies, and freelance developers. Each page leads with what brings in leads (page speed, structured data, and well-built forms) instead of price, because I'm not the cheapest option but aim to be the one that does the most for a service business.</li>
