      <h2 id="why-static">Why static instead of dynamic</h2>
      <p>The sites I build are small-business marketing sites, service-area pages, and blogs. None of them needs a database, a PHP runtime, or a Node server in the path of a page request. Every layer of runtime I remove is one that can't break, can't be hacked, and can't slow a page down.</p>

      <h3>WordPress</h3>
      <p>WordPress runs about 40% of the web, and its familiar admin panel and huge plugin library are real strengths. It runs on PHP and MySQL, and it's also the most-attacked web platform by a wide margin. Every plugin adds code that can be attacked, and every missed update leaves a vulnerability. Bloated themes, render-blocking scripts, and slow database queries make Core Web Vitals a constant fight. Even well-tuned sites struggle to reach the Good threshold without heavy caching plugins, and a page builder can undo half of that work.</p>
      <p>I don't build on WordPress, because the admin panel doesn't outweigh the upkeep, the recurring security holes, the speed ceiling, and the need for a PHP host at runtime. Clients don't miss the admin panel once they see they can email me what they want changed.</p>

      <h3>Next.js, Astro, SvelteKit, Remix</h3>
      <p>Modern meta-frameworks are excellent for application-shaped problems: user accounts, live state, interactive dashboards, and online stores with live inventory. For a six-to-twelve-page service-business site, they're more than the job needs. They add a JavaScript runtime the visitor never benefits from, they shift complexity onto the developer (and so onto the monthly bill), and they put a Node server or serverless platform in the request path, which brings cold starts, variable costs, and one more thing to monitor.</p>
      <p>If 11ty didn't exist, Astro is what I'd reach for, and its island architecture is elegant. I stay with 11ty because these sites don't need islands. They need clean HTML and CSS served from a CDN with a time to first byte under 100 ms, and 11ty produces exactly that with less abstraction.</p>

      <h3>Squarespace, Wix, Webflow, Framer</h3>
      <p>Hosted builders suit beginners and businesses that want a website without plans to grow it. The trade-offs are that the site lives on a proprietary platform, you pay $16 to $50 a month just to keep it online, and the platform's decisions set the limits on your SEO. Moving away usually means rebuilding.</p>

      <h2 id="performance">Speed targets and methods</h2>
      <p>On every page of every site, I aim for the Good threshold on every Core Web Vital, on both mobile and desktop. The targets:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Largest Contentful Paint (LCP):</strong> &lt; 1.0 s (Google's Good threshold is &lt; 2.5 s).</li>
        <li><strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS):</strong> &lt; 0.05 (Good: &lt; 0.1).</li>
        <li><strong>Interaction to Next Paint (INP):</strong> &lt; 100 ms (Good: &lt; 200 ms).</li>
        <li><strong>Total Blocking Time (TBT):</strong> &lt; 100 ms.</li>
        <li><strong>First Contentful Paint (FCP):</strong> &lt; 800 ms.</li>
        <li><strong>Speed Index:</strong> &lt; 1.5 s.</li>
        <li><strong>Time to First Byte (TTFB):</strong> &lt; 100 ms for any visitor in North America.</li>
        <li><strong>Google PageSpeed score:</strong> 95 to 100 on mobile and desktop.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Performance budget</h3>
      <p>Each page has an asset budget. If a deploy would go over it, the build fails until I trim the page or justify the extra weight.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>HTML: under 25 KB gzipped per page.</li>
        <li>CSS: one stylesheet, under 30 KB gzipped, shared across the whole site.</li>
        <li>JavaScript: under 10 KB gzipped per page (most pages use 4 to 7 KB).</li>
        <li>Hero image: under 80 KB as AVIF or 120 KB as WebP, at the width each visitor needs.</li>
        <li>Web fonts: two faces at most, subset to Latin unless the content needs more.</li>
        <li>Third-party scripts: none by default. Each one needs a written reason and a line in the budget.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>How I meet those numbers</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No render-blocking JavaScript.</strong> Every script uses <code>defer</code> or <code>type="module"</code>, and a typical page loads under 10 KB of gzipped JS.</li>
        <li><strong>Responsive images in AVIF, with WebP and JPEG fallbacks.</strong> Every image has explicit width and height to prevent layout shift, native <code>loading="lazy"</code> below the fold, and <code>decoding="async"</code> for progressive rendering.</li>
        <li><strong>System font fallbacks.</strong> A preconnect to Bunny Fonts plus <code>font-display: swap</code> means the page shows text in a system font if the custom fonts take more than 100 ms, so text never goes invisible while fonts load.</li>
        <li><strong>CSS joined at build time.</strong> One stylesheet means one HTTP request and no chain of <code>@import</code> requests. A content-hash query string (<code>?v=4a701c154dc9</code>) handles cache busting.</li>
        <li><strong>Minified HTML, CSS, and JS.</strong> Production builds run through <code>html-minifier-terser</code> with <code>minifyCSS</code> and <code>minifyJS</code> turned on.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare's edge.</strong> Static files come from the CDN location closest to the visitor, with HTTP/3, Brotli compression, and Early Hints where supported.</li>
        <li><strong>Long-term caching.</strong> Asset URLs are content-hashed, so files can be cached for a year and still update the moment they change.</li>
        <li><strong>No web fonts on the first lines of content.</strong> The hero falls back to system fonts, so the first paint always has readable text.</li>
        <li><strong>Preconnect to the font server.</strong> A single <code>preconnect</code> hint to <code>fonts.bunny.net</code> in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> saves about 200 ms on font loading for first-time visitors.</li>
        <li><strong>No critical-CSS extraction.</strong> The stylesheet is small enough (under 30 KB gzipped) to send in full, so the whole sheet arrives before the page renders.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="image-pipeline">Image processing</h2>
      <p>Images do more than anything else to slow a typical small-business site, so every image goes through the same process.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source format:</strong> I accept whatever the client sends (HEIC, RAW, JPEG, or PNG). The build converts each to JPEG internally, then writes AVIF, WebP, and JPEG versions.</li>
        <li><strong>Output formats:</strong> AVIF first, WebP as the fallback, and JPEG as the floor. Each image is a <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;source type="image/avif"&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;source type="image/webp"&gt;</code>, and an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> as the JPEG floor.</li>
        <li><strong>Responsive widths:</strong> 320, 480, 640, 800, 1200, 1600, and 2000 px. Each image's <code>srcset</code> lists at least four widths, and the browser picks one based on the viewport, pixel density, and bandwidth hints.</li>
        <li><strong>Layout stability:</strong> every <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> carries explicit <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> attributes from the source. The browser uses them to reserve space, so nothing shifts as images load.</li>
        <li><strong>Lazy loading:</strong> <code>loading="lazy"</code> on every image below the fold, and <code>decoding="async"</code> on all of them. The hero image loads eagerly with <code>fetchpriority="high"</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Quality settings:</strong> AVIF at q=70, WebP at q=80, and JPEG at q=82. I set these by comparing images side by side; below them, banding shows on photographs.</li>
        <li><strong>Naming:</strong> filenames are content-hashed, so cache busting is automatic. The same source always produces the same output names.</li>
        <li><strong>Typical savings:</strong> a 4 MB phone photo usually becomes a 60 KB AVIF, a 90 KB WebP, or a 140 KB JPEG at the largest size, with smaller versions down to about 12 KB for phones.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fonts">Fonts</h2>
      <p>Web fonts can stall a page. The browser's default behavior (invisible text, or a flash of unstyled text) is bad enough that I plan font loading as carefully as images.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Provider:</strong> <a href="https://fonts.bunny.net" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Bunny Fonts</a>, a GDPR-compliant mirror of Google Fonts with no cookies, no tracking, and no consent banner. A client who prefers it can have self-hosted woff2 files instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Preconnect:</strong> <code>&lt;link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.bunny.net"&gt;</code> in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> opens the connection before the CSS asks for the fonts.</li>
        <li><strong>Display:</strong> <code>font-display: swap</code>. The browser shows text in the system font at once, then swaps in the web font when it arrives.</li>
        <li><strong>Subsetting:</strong> Latin only by default. Adding a subset is one line in the URL but doubles the file size, so I add one only when the content needs it.</li>
        <li><strong>Faces:</strong> two families at most (one display, one body), each with no more than three weights. I use variable fonts when the family offers them.</li>
        <li><strong>System fallbacks:</strong> every font declaration ends in a system stack (<code>system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif</code>), so the first paint is always readable.</li>
        <li><strong>Cache:</strong> Bunny serves fonts with a year-long cache, so returning visitors don't download them again.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="css-architecture">CSS, design, and layout</h2>
      <p>Each site has one stylesheet of vanilla CSS, built from partials and custom properties. There's no framework, preprocessor, CSS-in-JS, or PostCSS.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Partials</strong> live in <code>src/assets/css/</code>, sorted by job: <code>reset.css</code>, <code>tokens.css</code>, <code>utilities.css</code>, <code>components/*.css</code>, and <code>pages/*.css</code>. Each file handles one job.</li>
        <li><strong>The entry point</strong> (<code>global.css</code>) is mostly a comment. A custom Eleventy template extension reads each partial in a set order with <code>fs.readFileSync</code> and writes one joined, minified file.</li>
        <li><strong>No <code>@import</code>:</strong> the browser holds rendering for every <code>@import</code> chain, which creates a request waterfall. Joining the files at build time avoids it.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom properties throughout.</strong> Color, type scale, spacing, radius, shadow, transition, and breakpoint values all live in <code>tokens.css</code>. The light and dark themes change the values, not the components.</li>
        <li><strong>Fluid type and spacing</strong> with <code>clamp()</code>. Most type tokens are <code>clamp(min, preferred, max)</code>, so they scale with the viewport without breakpoints.</li>
        <li><strong>Container queries</strong> for components that respond to their parent instead of the viewport, mostly cards in flexible grids.</li>
        <li><strong>Cascade layers</strong> (<code>@layer reset, tokens, utilities, components, pages</code>) keep specificity predictable, so a late utility never outranks a component by accident.</li>
        <li><strong>Cache busting:</strong> the stylesheet carries a content-hash query string (<code>style.css?v=4a701c154dc9</code>), so each deploy refreshes the cache for returning visitors all at once.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Design system</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Type:</strong> a custom-paired display face and body face from Bunny Fonts, or self-hosted woff2 files if you prefer.</li>
        <li><strong>Color:</strong> one primary color, one accent, and three or four neutral grays, all meeting WCAG AA contrast.</li>
        <li><strong>Grid:</strong> a 12-column responsive grid with fluid type (<code>clamp()</code>), so the design scales from a 360 px phone to a 1440 px desktop.</li>
        <li><strong>Corners:</strong> square by default for a professional look, and rounded only when the brand calls for warmth.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Phones and tablets</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mobile-first:</strong> every layout starts at phone size and adapts cleanly across the common breakpoints (360, 414, 568, 736, 768, 820, 1024, 1180, and 1280 px).</li>
        <li><strong>Tablets:</strong> portrait (768 to 820 px) and landscape (1024 to 1180 px) get explicit attention, because they fall in a gap most sites ignore.</li>
        <li><strong>Touch targets:</strong> 44x44 px at minimum.</li>
        <li><strong>Form inputs:</strong> 16 px type, so iOS doesn't zoom in when a visitor taps a field.</li>
        <li><strong>Sticky headers</strong> that behave as the iOS Safari address bar collapses.</li>
        <li><strong>Phones in landscape</strong> (568 to 736 px wide and about 360 px tall) get shorter hero padding, so the buttons stay visible without scrolling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="js-architecture">JavaScript</h2>
      <p>JavaScript makes a few interactions nicer, and the site works fully without it. In detail:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No framework, router, or state library.</strong> Every script is a small, self-contained module.</li>
        <li><strong>Deferred and module scripts.</strong> Every script loads with <code>defer</code> or as <code>type="module"</code>, so none blocks parsing.</li>
        <li><strong>An inline theme script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.</strong> It reads <code>localStorage</code> and <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> and sets <code>data-theme</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> before paint, which prevents a flash of the wrong theme.</li>
        <li><strong>Inline event handlers as a backup</strong> for the menu toggle, search, and theme toggle. The minifier can occasionally break event-listener binding on deferred scripts, so inline <code>onclick</code> attributes act as a safety net.</li>
        <li><strong>IntersectionObserver for the blog contents list.</strong> The sticky table of contents highlights the current section as the visitor scrolls, with no scroll-event listeners.</li>
        <li><strong>Pagefind UI</strong>: a small ESM module bundled by Pagefind, loaded only on pages with a <code>#search</code> container.</li>
        <li><strong>Reading progress bar, copy-link buttons, and share buttons</strong> on blog posts, all vanilla JS and under 2 KB combined.</li>
        <li><strong>No tracking pixels</strong> and no third-party advertising scripts. Analytics is server-side or first-party.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The blog template</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A paginated blog index, with a featured article at the top of page one.</li>
        <li>A post template with a fixed reading-progress bar at the top, a sticky table of contents in the sidebar built automatically from the headings, and a drop cap on the first paragraph.</li>
        <li>An author card with a photo, and a share row with four buttons (X, LinkedIn, email, and copy link).</li>
        <li>Previous and next links between posts, a strip of related articles, an RSS feed, and BlogPosting JSON-LD on every post so it can qualify for rich results.</li>
        <li>You write each post in plain text or a Google Doc, and I lay it out and publish it for you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="theming">Themes and dark mode</h2>
      <p>Light and dark themes are built in from the start. Theme tokens live in <code>tokens.css</code> as CSS custom properties, and <code>:root[data-theme="dark"]</code> overrides the values. Components use tokens, never raw colors.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Toggle:</strong> a segmented control with three states (<em>Light</em>, <em>System</em>, and <em>Dark</em>). System follows <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> as it changes.</li>
        <li><strong>Memory:</strong> the visitor's choice is saved to <code>localStorage</code> and applied before paint on the next visit.</li>
        <li><strong>No flash:</strong> a tiny inline script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> reads the saved choice and sets <code>data-theme</code> before the body renders. Without it, the page would briefly show the wrong theme.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduced motion:</strong> a global rule turns off transitions and animations when <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> is set. I test it with the operating-system setting turned on.</li>
        <li><strong>Forced colors:</strong> I test in Windows High Contrast mode and honor system colors where needed through <code>forced-colors: active</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Color-scheme meta:</strong> <code>&lt;meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark"&gt;</code> tells the browser both themes are supported, so form controls and scrollbars adapt on their own.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="search">Site search</h2>
      <p>Pagefind builds a static index at build time. Search runs entirely in the visitor's browser, with no server, no API, and no cookies.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Index size:</strong> usually 50 to 250 KB in all for a small-business site, served as small JSON chunks fetched as needed.</li>
        <li><strong>What's indexed:</strong> every page in <code>collections.all</code> except excluded paths (the style guide, brand guidelines, sitemap, and similar pages). Front-matter <code>tags</code> become filters when I connect them.</li>
        <li><strong>Build hook:</strong> Pagefind runs in an <code>eleventy.after</code> hook, so the default Cloudflare Pages preset (which runs <code>npx @11ty/eleventy</code> directly) picks it up without a custom command. The site is re-indexed on every deploy.</li>
        <li><strong>Interface:</strong> Pagefind's standard UI module, styled to match the site and loaded only on pages with a <code>#search</code> container.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> the slash key opens search, and Escape closes it.</li>
        <li><strong>Speed:</strong> the search box is ready in well under 100 ms, because the bundle is small and the index loads lazily.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="forms">Forms</h2>
      <p>I don't run a form server. Every form on every site I build posts straight to <a href="https://web3forms.com" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">Web3Forms</a>, which sends the submission to the email address or addresses the client chooses.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Submission:</strong> the form POSTs <code>multipart/form-data</code> to <code>https://api.web3forms.com/submit</code> with the client's access key. Web3Forms validates it, applies rate limits, and emails the client.</li>
        <li><strong>Spam protection:</strong> a hidden honeypot field (<code>name="botcheck"</code>) catches simple bots, and Web3Forms' rate limits and filtering catch the smarter ones. I don't use a CAPTCHA, because it costs conversions and bots mostly get past it anyway.</li>
        <li><strong>Validation:</strong> native HTML5 (<code>required</code>, <code>type="email"</code>, and <code>pattern</code>). Errors are accessible: an invalid field gets <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> and a visible message announced through <code>role="alert"</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>On success:</strong> the page redirects to <code>/thank-you/?type=&lt;form-name&gt;</code>, which reads the type and shows next steps for that form.</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-reply:</strong> the visitor gets an email right away with the client's phone number, so they have something to act on.</li>
        <li><strong>On failure:</strong> after a network error, the form shows an inline message (<code>#form-error</code>) with a phone number and email address, so the visitor can still reach the business.</li>
        <li><strong>Where the data lives:</strong> in the client's inbox. I never store submissions on infrastructure I control, and I don't see a message unless the client forwards it to me.</li>
        <li><strong>Trade-specific intake:</strong> forms can ask for the details a job needs, such as address, scope, and urgency for a plumbing emergency, roof age and insurance carrier for roofing, or BTU and SEER specs for HVAC.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond Web3Forms:</strong> for routing logic, fields that change by ZIP code, or a CRM connection, I use a Cloudflare Worker (a serverless edge function). There's still no traditional server.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="seo">SEO setup</h2>
      <p>SEO has two halves: the technical foundation, which a site either has or doesn't, and ongoing content, which builds over time. I build the foundation, and the content is yours.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Per-page metadata.</strong> A title and meta description (50 to 160 characters, naming the service and city), a canonical URL, and Open Graph and Twitter Card tags. Each is set in front matter or layout defaults, and none is duplicated across pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Structured data (JSON-LD).</strong> <code>WebSite</code>, <code>LocalBusiness</code>, and <code>BreadcrumbList</code> on every page, <code>Service</code> on each service page, <code>BlogPosting</code> on each post, <code>FAQPage</code> on the FAQ, and <code>Person</code> on author pages. All of it is checked against schema.org and Google's Rich Results Test before launch.</li>
        <li><strong>Heading order.</strong> One <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> per page, then headings in descending order with no skipped levels. Page sections use <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> with a heading.</li>
        <li><strong>Sitemap.</strong> A custom <code>sitemap.njk</code> writes <code>sitemap.xml</code> by looping through <code>collections.all</code>. I submit it to Google Search Console at launch, and it refreshes on every deploy.</li>
        <li><strong>robots.txt.</strong> It allows everything by default and points to the sitemap, so nothing is blocked by accident.</li>
        <li><strong>Internal links.</strong> Service-area pages link to neighboring areas, and service pages link to related services. Every page is two clicks or fewer from the home page.</li>
        <li><strong>Clean URLs.</strong> Trailing slash, lowercase, hyphenated, and never abbreviated: <code>/services/water-heater-install/</code>, not <code>/svcs/whInstall</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Mobile-first.</strong> Google indexes the mobile version of a site first, and my designs start with mobile too.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Standard files and pages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Files for search engines and browsers:</strong> <code>sitemap.xml</code>, <code>robots.txt</code>, an RSS feed if the site has a blog, <code>security.txt</code>, a branded 404 page, a full favicon set for every device and browser, and Open Graph and Twitter Card images that show when someone shares a link in Slack or iMessage.</li>
        <li><strong>Operational pages written for each business:</strong> a privacy policy that names the site's subprocessors, an accessibility statement that reflects the site's actual conformance, and a style guide that sets out the visual standard, which the client can hand to a print shop or sign painter.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Service-area pages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One data file:</strong> Eleventy pagination builds every area page from a single data file.</li>
        <li><strong>Local content:</strong> each page gets its own local detail (specific streets, ZIP codes, and landmarks), links to nearby areas, breadcrumbs, a sidebar call to action, and LocalBusiness schema with the area's coordinates.</li>
        <li><strong>Easy to add:</strong> a new area is a 10-line change that builds and deploys in 60 seconds. I've launched sites with more than 30 area pages.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility testing</h2>
      <p>My working standard is <span class="term" data-term="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, version 2.2, Level AA: the W3C's accessibility standard for web content.">WCAG 2.2 Level AA</span>. I test every site against it before launch and again after every significant change. The methods:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Automated audits:</strong> Lighthouse (in Chrome DevTools), <a href="https://www.deque.com/axe/devtools/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">axe DevTools</a>, and <a href="https://wave.webaim.org" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">WAVE</a>. I aim for zero critical and zero serious issues.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyboard-only navigation</strong> from the first focusable element to the last, on every page. Every interactive control must be reachable and usable.</li>
        <li><strong>Screen-reader testing:</strong> VoiceOver on macOS and iOS, and NVDA on Windows. I listen to every page from top to bottom to catch problems with landmarks, headings, and labels.</li>
        <li><strong>Contrast checks</strong> with the <a href="https://www.tpgi.com/color-contrast-checker/" class="inline-link" rel="noopener">TPGi Colour Contrast Analyser</a>, against 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text and interface parts. I check every text and background pair in both light and dark modes.</li>
        <li><strong>Zoom testing:</strong> up to 200% browser zoom with no sideways scrolling and no clipped content.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduced motion:</strong> every animation and transition turns off when <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> is set. I test it with the operating-system setting turned on.</li>
        <li><strong>Forced colors:</strong> pages stay readable in Windows High Contrast mode, and I use <code>forced-colors: active</code> media queries where a component's styling needs to give way to system colors.</li>
        <li><strong>Touch targets:</strong> at least 44x44 CSS pixels on every interactive element.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What that means in practice</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Semantic HTML: proper <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> landmarks, headings in order, lists that are lists, and buttons that are buttons.</li>
        <li>A skip-to-content link as the first focusable element.</li>
        <li>A visible focus ring (a 3 px gold ring at a 3 px offset) on every interactive control.</li>
        <li>Tap targets of at least 44x44 CSS pixels.</li>
        <li>Form labels tied to their inputs with <code>for</code> and <code>id</code>, and errors announced through <code>role="alert"</code>.</li>
        <li>Radio and checkbox groups wrapped in <code>role="radiogroup"</code> or <code>role="group"</code>, with <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing to the visible label.</li>
        <li>Decorative SVGs marked <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> and <code>focusable="false"</code>, and meaningful images given real <code>alt</code> text.</li>
        <li>Information that never depends on color alone.</li>
        <li><code>aria-current="page"</code> on the active menu link, so screen readers announce where the visitor is.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
      <p>Code that doesn't exist can't be hacked. Staying static keeps the attack surface close to zero.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No server-side runtime.</strong> There's no PHP, no Node runtime, and no application server in the request path, so there's nothing open to remote code execution, no database to SQL-inject, and no session store to hijack.</li>
        <li><strong>No plugins.</strong> WordPress plugins have produced CVEs for more than a decade. My sites have none.</li>
        <li><strong>No database.</strong> There are no credentials to leak, no database backups to manage, and no personal data kept on infrastructure I control. Form submissions go straight to the client's email.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTPS from day one.</strong> Cloudflare manages the SSL certificates automatically. HSTS headers carry <code>preload</code> and <code>includeSubDomains</code>, with a 12-month max-age.</li>
        <li><strong>Content Security Policy and security headers</strong>, set in <code>_headers</code> on every site:
          <ul>
            <li><code>Content-Security-Policy</code> limited to known origins.</li>
            <li><code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code> and <code>frame-ancestors 'none'</code>.</li>
            <li><code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>.</li>
            <li><code>Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code>.</li>
            <li><code>Permissions-Policy</code> that denies the camera, microphone, geolocation, FLoC, and other powerful APIs by default.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>security.txt</code></strong> on every site, so researchers know how to report a vulnerability responsibly.</li>
        <li><strong>Dependabot</strong> on every repository, for alerts about npm package vulnerabilities. There are only a handful of build-time dev dependencies and no runtime dependencies, but I track them.</li>
        <li><strong>Signed commits.</strong> Every commit is GPG-signed and verified by GitHub.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="caching">Caching and invalidation</h2>
      <p>Cloudflare's edge caches static files aggressively. I make sure every deploy updates the cache all at once, so a visitor never gets a page that's half old and half new.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>HTML:</strong> sent with <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate</code>, so the edge checks for a new version on every request. Cloudflare returns a 304 if nothing changed, and the round trip takes under 50 ms.</li>
        <li><strong>CSS and JS:</strong> sent with <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code>. The URLs include a content-hash query string, so a new deploy replaces returning visitors' cached copies without serving stale files.</li>
        <li><strong>Images:</strong> the same <code>immutable</code> policy. Filenames are content-hashed, so no two versions collide.</li>
        <li><strong>Fonts:</strong> served by Bunny with a year-long cache.</li>
        <li><strong>Search index:</strong> Pagefind chunks are content-hashed and cached as immutable.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="build">Build and deploy</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Commit.</strong> I review each change and commit it to the <code>main</code> branch on GitHub, with every commit signed.</li>
        <li><strong>CI trigger.</strong> Cloudflare Pages sees the push and starts a clean build container.</li>
        <li><strong>Build.</strong> <code>npx @11ty/eleventy</code> compiles the templates, turns images into responsive AVIF, WebP, and JPEG versions, generates the favicons, and writes the RSS feed, <code>sitemap.xml</code>, and the HTML sitemap.</li>
        <li><strong>Minification.</strong> In production, HTML, CSS, and JS run through <code>html-minifier-terser</code> with careful settings that keep whitespace where it matters.</li>
        <li><strong>Search indexing.</strong> Pagefind runs in an <code>eleventy.after</code> hook, so the Cloudflare Pages preset works without changes, and writes the search chunks to <code>/pagefind/*</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Cache busting.</strong> <code>eleventy-auto-cache-buster</code> adds a content-hash query string to every stylesheet and script reference, so returning visitors get updates at once.</li>
        <li><strong>Deploy.</strong> Cloudflare pushes the build to its global edge in under 60 seconds. Every branch other than <code>main</code> gets a preview URL, so I can share work in progress with the client.</li>
        <li><strong>Rollback.</strong> Every earlier deploy stays available, and if anything is wrong I can roll back in one click. Reverting a change in git and redeploying takes under five minutes from start to finish. There's no database to back up, because there's no database.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="environments">Environments and branches</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>main</code></strong>: production. It deploys to the live URL on every push.</li>
        <li><strong>Feature branches</strong>: any branch name. Each one deploys to <code>&lt;branch&gt;.&lt;project&gt;.pages.dev</code>, which I use for client review of changes in progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Pull requests</strong>: each gets its own preview URL and a link posted back in a comment. I share these with clients before merging.</li>
        <li><strong>Local development</strong>: <code>npm run dev</code> or <code>npm start</code>. The commands work on every platform (no <code>ELEVENTY_ENV=</code> prefix, which only works in bash), with hot reload from 11ty's built-in server.</li>
        <li><strong>Environment variables</strong>: <code>SITE_URL</code> has a sensible default that each environment can override. Secrets such as Web3Forms keys and analytics IDs live in environment variables, never in the repository.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="measurement">Measurement and monitoring</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Core Web Vitals</strong> through Cloudflare's server-side RUM: real data from every visitor, gathered without cookies.</li>
        <li><strong>Uptime monitoring</strong> through Cloudflare's status API, plus an outside monitor (UptimeRobot) that checks every minute from three regions and texts me if a site goes down. I hear about an outage before the client notices.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Search Console</strong>, connected at launch for every client, so I can see real search impressions, clicks, and Core Web Vitals reports from Google's own crawl data.</li>
        <li><strong>Bing Webmaster Tools</strong>, which is underrated: Bing still brings in about 10 to 15% of service-business traffic, depending on the industry.</li>
        <li><strong>Lighthouse CI</strong> on major changes, to catch drops in speed or accessibility before they go live.</li>
        <li><strong>Form monitoring:</strong> Web3Forms reports submission counts, and if a form goes quiet for 10 days, I email the client to check that it still works.</li>
        <li><strong>Domain expiration monitoring:</strong> I watch WHOIS for every client domain I manage, so renewal reminders don't depend on the registrar's email.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="browser-support">Browser support</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tier 1 (full support):</strong> the latest two versions of Chrome, Edge, Safari, and Firefox, on desktop and mobile.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 2 (graceful degradation):</strong> Chromium-based browsers up to two years old. CSS custom properties, Grid, Flexbox, container queries (with a fallback), and the AVIF and WebP images all work.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 3 (works, but plain):</strong> very old browsers from the Internet Explorer era get unstyled, semantic HTML. The site is still readable, just without the typography and spacing.</li>
        <li><strong>No JavaScript:</strong> every page works without it. Search and the theme toggle degrade gracefully, and everything else (menus, forms, and content) works the same.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduced data and reduced motion:</strong> both respected through media queries.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="failure-modes">What can fail, and what I do about it</h2>
      <p>Every system can fail somewhere. Here's where mine can, and how I handle each case:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A Web3Forms outage</strong> would stop form submissions until it recovers. The page shows a fallback message with a phone number and email address, so visitors can still get through. In my experience, Web3Forms' uptime is 99.95% or better, and no incident has lasted more than 30 minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>A regional Cloudflare outage</strong> would affect visitors in that region only. I watch Cloudflare's status feed and post a note on Twitter/X if I see one. Visitors elsewhere aren't affected.</li>
        <li><strong>A Bunny Fonts outage</strong> means the site shows its system fonts, the design's second-best look, which is still readable and on-brand, with no layout shift.</li>
        <li><strong>DNS mistakes during a registrar transfer</strong> are the biggest risk in any site move. I plan transfers carefully and use a five-minute TTL during the change, so I can roll back fast.</li>
        <li><strong>An expired client domain</strong> would take the site offline within 24 to 48 hours. I watch the WHOIS expiration date directly and remind clients at 60, 30, and 7 days.</li>
        <li><strong>A typo committed to <code>main</code></strong> would go live. Pull-request previews, my own checks before each deploy, and one-click rollback guard against it. The longest a typo has ever stayed live on a production site of mine is 90 seconds, and it was in a footer copyright line.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="faq">Technical questions</h2>

      <h3>Why 11ty over Astro?</h3>
      <p>Astro is a great tool, and if it had existed when I started, I might have picked it. Today, for service-business marketing sites, 11ty has less abstraction, a steadier plugin ecosystem for my needs, and the same output. I'd consider Astro case by case for a project that benefits from islands, and most of my builds don't.</p>

      <h3>Why no JavaScript framework?</h3>
      <p>My sites don't have application state: no shopping cart, no multi-page form with shared validation, and no dashboard. A few hundred lines of vanilla JavaScript, added as progressive enhancement, cover every interaction on them: the theme toggle, mobile menu, FAQ accordion, form submission, site search, and glossary tooltips. A framework would add 30 to 100 KB of runtime and make me manage hydration, which is a large cost for no benefit.</p>

      <h3>How do you handle a blog with 200 or more posts?</h3>
      <p>11ty handles it well, and I've done it. Build time grows roughly in line with the number of posts, and a 200-post blog builds in under 10 seconds on Cloudflare's CI. Incremental builds (the <code>--incremental</code> flag) take under a second locally while writing. Pagefind indexes the whole blog in milliseconds, because it reads the finished HTML, not the source templates.</p>

      <h3>What if I need a contact form that does more than send email?</h3>
      <p>Web3Forms covers about 95% of small-business form needs. For the other 5% (CRM connections, routing by ZIP code, and conditional steps), I use Cloudflare Workers, which are serverless edge functions, so there's still no server to maintain. For anything that's really an application (booking calendars, live chat, or client portals with accounts), I connect a service built for the job, such as Calendly or Tawk.to, instead of building one from scratch.</p>

      <h3>Can I see the source code before I buy?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The site you're reading is a fair sample of what I build, and you can view its source in your browser. I'm glad to share this site's GitHub repository on request.</p>

      <h3>What do you do that a typical agency doesn't?</h3>
      <p>Nothing exotic. I put more time into measurement (Lighthouse CI, real-user monitoring, and hands-on accessibility testing) than into new tools. The right tools for a marketing site have been stable for five years, and I've chosen to use them with discipline.</p>

      <h3>Can I run my own hosting?</h3>
      <p>Not on the monthly plan. The built site is plain static files that any CDN could serve (Netlify, Vercel, S3 with CloudFront, GitHub Pages, or your own nginx). On the plan, though, I own the code and host it on Cloudflare Pages, which costs nothing and has an excellent edge network. Your domain stays registered in your name, and you control it.</p>

      <h3>Can I move off your setup later?</h3>
      <p>If you cancel, I help you move your domain to a registrar of your choice, and the site goes offline at the end of the term. On the monthly plan I own the code, so there's no export of the site and no code buyout. Your photos, text, and brand assets stay yours.</p>

      <h3>Do you do A/B testing?</h3>
      <p>Yes, in a lightweight way. I deploy two versions of a page at different URLs and split traffic with a Cloudflare Worker. I don't add Optimizely or VWO unless the testing volume justifies the extra weight, and for a small business it almost never does.</p>

      <h3>How do you handle content from visitors?</h3>
      <p>By default, I don't. If a client needs comments, reviews, or visitor submissions on the public site, I connect a service built for it (Disqus, GoRated, or Google Reviews through its API) instead of running my own moderation. Content from visitors brings a different kind of upkeep than static publishing, so I treat it as a separate feature.</p>

      <h3>What's the weak point of this approach?</h3>
      <p>Frequent changes by non-technical staff. If a client needs to edit content in a browser (say, a 30-person team where everyone writes blog posts), emailing me each change won't keep up. In that case I add a headless CMS (Sanity or Decap), or I recommend a firm that specializes in WordPress or a full CMS. Most small businesses don't have this problem, and most of their "CMS needs" are better met by three emails a month to the person who built the site.</p>

      <h3>Can I see your build config?</h3>
      <p>Yes. <code>eleventy.config.js</code> is the entry point in each of my repositories, and I share it on request. It holds no secrets, because secrets live in environment variables.</p>

